# The `hardened` POSTURE — one variable, and the product does the rest.
#
# Load it with whatever your platform uses (docs/environments.md "Loaders"):
#   docker compose --env-file deploy/env/posture/hardened.env …
#   set -a; . deploy/env/posture/hardened.env; . deploy/secrets.env; set +a; java -jar app.jar
#   kubectl create configmap dp-posture --from-env-file=deploy/env/posture/hardened.env
#   systemd:  EnvironmentFile=/etc/datapipelines/hardened.env
#
# WHAT THE POSTURE MEANS (the normative table lives in docs/environments.md and
# docs/configuration.md §3.23; these are its `hardened` column):
#
#   authoring-enabled     FALSE     — a promotion RECEIVER by default
#   demo (DATAPIPELINES_DEMO)       REFUSED at boot
#   local bootstrap password        REFUSED at boot (local accounts stay allowed)
#   loopback metadata DB / Redis    REFUSED at boot
#   session cookie Secure           ON
#   OIDC                            REQUIRED unless DATAPIPELINES_AUTH_ALLOW_LOCAL_ONLY=true
#
# The DEFAULTS are carried by the image's `application-hardened.yml`, which this
# variable selects; the REFUSALS are ConfigValidator §7 rules, which no file can carry.
# Nothing is repeated here as an env var on purpose: a second copy of a default is a
# second authority. Every one is still an ordinary variable you may set — in
# deploy/secrets.env, your Secret, or the shell:
#
#   DATAPIPELINES_DEPLOYMENT_AUTHORING_ENABLED=true   # a one-box deployment that authors
#   DATAPIPELINES_AUTH_ALLOW_LOCAL_ONLY=true          # no OIDC; local accounts only
#
# You must also set DATAPIPELINES_ENV to YOUR name for this deployment (`qa`, `uat`,
# `prod`, `sandbox-eu` — any name): a named environment with no posture, or a posture
# with no name, is exactly what this pair exists to make explicit.
#
# deploy/env/example.env is the full reference of every variable.
DATAPIPELINES_POSTURE=hardened
